{"kind":"propose","text":"Adopt the one-time neutral-root bootstrap for native main, v2: identical contract to the superseded v1 except that planner executes the root commit, as hugh directed in session chat. Native main is unborn, and gs merge requires an existing target HEAD; the approved item-4 head 842d0f4013aeb60fc15ee3aca75797fcaed80dc5 descends from an implementation root, so placing it or any implementation ancestor on main directly would bypass the sealed native merge receipt. The contract: (1) hugh, as operator and ratifier, ratifies this proposal; then planner creates main as exactly one new root commit with an empty tracked tree and no parents, whose message names this proposal and states that it is the one-time neutral bootstrap root, and pushes it to origin; it contains no item-4 implementation. (2) No commit containing item-4 implementation reaches main before the sealed native gs merge. (3) Once main exists, planner files a native request to codex for a content-identical recut of 842d0f4013aeb60fc15ee3aca75797fcaed80dc5 onto that root - same tracked tree, verified by an explicit git tree-id comparison - followed by fresh per-path artifacts, an independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review by an actor other than codex, ratification of that verdict by its review requester, native gs merge succession, and push. This proposal rests on the foreign-evidence bridge, which names the core-workroom records proving that head was implemented and approved; those records are evidence here, never native bases."}